 of  30 are being questioned as a part of the  investigation our inquiries indicate  that an improvised explosive device has  been manufactured and our assumption so  far is that it was built by the  passenger in the taxi  the reason why he then took it to the  women's hospital is unknown  david perry has been identified by  british media as the taxi driver in  security video he can be seen jumping  out of the vehicle he escaped serious  injury what yesterday showed above all  is that the british people will never be  cowed by terrorism we will never give in  to those who seek to divide us with  senseless acts of violence the terror  threat alert in the uk is now at the  second highest level severe it's a  warning to the public that attacks are  highly likely sunday's explosion was the  second alleged terrorist attack in the  u.k in the past month  in october british mp david amos was  stabbed to death while meeting with  constituents  we as a government i as home secretary  continue to work with everyone when it  comes to the security of our country  making sure that we're taking all the  necessary steps required police  identified the man killed in the taxi  blast as 32 year old imad  they say their investigation is still  active and officers are working at  multiple scenes  crystal gomanson global news london  in washington steve bannon former chief  strategist for donald trump has appeared  in court on contempt charges bannon  turned himself in after refusing to  cooperate with the investigation into  the january sixth attack on the u.s  capitol he's not being kept in custody  and will be arraigned on thursday if  convicted bannon faces up to a year in  prison for each charge it's not clear if  the prosecution will sway any of the  other 35 witnesses who've been  subpoenaed but have refused to cooperate  beefing up sanctions against belarus  coming up the eu's next move as the  migrant crisis deepens plus from happy  feet to aching feet one penguin's  journey crossing continents  [Music]  the air over india's capital is so toxic  schools and construction sites have been  shut down for four days the country's  supreme court also ordered offices to  close so people can work from home the  amount of hazardous particulate matter  in the air is six times the global  safety level coal-fired power plants are  one of the main reasons for the heavy  smog along with farmers burning crop  waste  the european union is stepping up  sanctions on belarus in response to an  escalating migrant crisis on the border  with poland the sanctions target a  number of individuals and entities for  facilitating illegal border crossings  into the eu the eu accuses president  alexander lukashenka of pushing migrants  towards its borders to undermine  security a charge he denies most of the  migrants caught in the middle of the  diplomatic standoff are now stranded  along the border in freezing conditions  with limited food and shelter  and the head of nato has warned russia  about its military buildup along the  border with ukraine  know that russia has been willing to use  these types of military capabilities  before  to conduct aggressive actions against  ukraine  yen stoltenberg called on russia to be  transparent about its activities and to  prevent any further escalation of  tensions the ukrainian government claims  nearly one hundred thousand russian  troops are amassing across the border it  says its own military forces are ready  to repel any possible acts of russian  aggression the kremlin dismissed claims  it's planning any sort of attack as  inflammatory  ahead how the supply crunch is holding  back the recovery of some small  businesses  supply chain issues are affecting  businesses of all kinds especially  retailers who rely on imports zan  gaviola explains big companies tend to  be more flexible and resilient than  small and medium-sized ones who are  worried about what the holiday season  might bring  november was nerve-racking for the dutch  growers garden center in saskatoon some  of their holiday stock was on board a  cargo ship that lost 109 containers off  the coast of bc followed by a fire on  board now their situation was unusual  but their predicament waiting on holiday  merchandise was not retailers across  canada have been literally sitting on  pins and needles  waiting since august  when are my shipments going to come how  much are they going to cost  as a medium-sized business with a  handful of locations the garden center  has been able to pivot to local goods  but even that strategy has its limits  when it comes closer to christmas you're  going to start to see the shelves  getting thinner and thinner the supply  chain crisis impacts goods made overseas  that come to our shores by boat there  are issues at every link in the chain  from manufacturing to shipping and  trucking larger businesses and  multinationals with deep pockets have  more options than small and medium-sized  companies such as chartering their own  cargo ships and even buying part of a  shipping port smaller firms don't have  the same bargaining or purchasing power  dan kelly represents the interests of 95  000 independently owned businesses  across the country the toll is still  being felt from nearly two years of  pandemic restrictions while big box  rivals were allowed to stay open and  reap record profits  one in six small businesses will  permanently close their doors as a  result of the damage that they've  sustained during the pandemic  independent business owner julie fass  says only the most nimble can survive  ever changing coveted rules labor  shortages cost increases and the supply  crunch it really has been  miraculous in some ways that we can keep  pivoting her shop sells things that  people like to touch and smell before  buying now she hopes people will shop  local and support homegrown  entrepreneurs trying to recover  it does take a bit of an effort  but i know  everyone wants to see these small  businesses succeed rather than flocking  to big box stores or amazon even if it  means more effort and more money ann  gaviola global news toronto  next the odyssey of a rare eagle from  russia to nova scotia  this little penguin is a long way from  home it's a natalie penguin and was  found on a new zealand beach at least 3  000 kilometers away from antarctica its  natural habitat locals named it pingu  and after some rehabilitation and food  it was released back onto a beach just  east of christchurch new zealand it's  only the third adelie penguin that has  been found on new zealand's shores  in nova scotia bird watchers have been  captivated by an eagle that's normally  spotted half a world away it's a  steller's sea eagle a rare arctic bird  that can be bigger than a bald eagle and  is typically seen in china japan and  korea and the east coast of russia as  ross lord explains bird watchers are  flocking to catch a glimpse of this  winged wanderer  so the white bear is on what we call the  leading edge of the wing when diane  leblanc found out this bird had been  seen in nova scotia she took flight out  the door the first immediate response  when you hear like that there's a  stellar sea eagle in nova scotia is a  heart-pounding  bird emergency feeling where you feel  like you have to just move as quickly as  you can she drove immediately to the  community of falmouth an hour away  there she joined an exclusive group of  bird lovers seeing and photographing the  steller's sea eagle a magnificent bird  of prey with a different appearance than  the more commonly seen bald eagle we  could definitely see the markings those  that bright orange bill and the white on  the sides of the of the wings  the shoulders with a wingspan of two and  a half meters and weighing up to nine  kilograms the stellars is the largest  eagle in the world as heavy as an  average one-year-old boy it seems the  eagle has been away from home for a long  time  seen in alaska last august possibly  swooping down over south texas this  spring  then turning up in quebec and de  brunswick in july before causing a stir  in nova scotia this month  that's almost 11 000 kilometers from its  native range in china and other parts of  asia and europe it's somewhere in  between extremely unusual and one in a  million  the thing about you know when when a  bird like this shows up  it certainly was not on  a lot of people's uh list so to speak as  the next super rare thing to show up the  stuller's eagle is declining in number  wildlife groups consider it a vulnerable  species number 353 for diane leblanc the  stellars is a precious entry in her life  list record of confirmed bird sightings  she hopes the eagle can make a life for  itself in north america or even better  finds a way to fly back home  ross lord global news windsor nova  scotia  and that is global national for this  monday i'm donna friesen tonight's your  canada is another bird it's a common  nighthawk in regina once abundant it is  now threatened we'd love to see your  canada please email it to viewers at  globalnational.com  thanks for watching hope to see you here  again tomorrow bye  [Music]
 

